    help: {
      body: [
        '<h3>1 · Frame, design, grid</h3>',
        '<p>On the right under “Zones” switch on the header band, the nav bar, the filters (panel left/right, bar on top or burger menu) and the footer (or use the gear or double-click on the zone). Under “Design” set the canvas size (HD, Full HD, Ultra HD), spacing, corners, tile style, background, colours and typography. Groups collapse and remember their state. Fonts scale along.</p>',
        '<h3>2 · Pages</h3>',
        '<p>The pages sit above the drawing area. “+ Page” adds one, a double-click renames it, duplicating and deleting live under “Page”. A visual can point to a target page (element → “Jumps to”); that is exported as drill / navigation.</p>',
        '<h3>3 · Splitting, merging and re-dividing containers</h3>',
        '<p>Every tile can be split into columns or rows with <kbd>⇔</kbd> / <kbd>⇕</kbd> and removed with <kbd>✕</kbd>. Drag the dividers to change the ratios.</p>',
        '<p><b>Merging:</b> In the gutter between two tiles the <kbd>+</kbd> joins the neighbours. An empty tile is absorbed by its neighbour; with two tiles the left or upper one keeps its visual. Dragging the gutter only moves the divider. <b>Edge plus:</b> The <kbd>+</kbd> at the edge of a level inserts a new tile there; all tiles of that level are then distributed evenly (two equal tiles become three equal tiles). <b>Re-split content:</b> Under “Page”, “Re-split content …” resets the content area to a grid of rows and columns. Existing visuals return to the new cells in order, surplus ones are lost.</p>',
        '<h3>4 · Visual, fields, notes</h3>',
        '<p>Double-click a tile → choose the type. Drag fields from the model onto the tile or onto a specific data role. Dragging a tile onto another tile swaps their contents. Notes on a tile show up as a ✎ icon on the card and on hover. If a measure is missing: create it via “+ Measure / dimension” with a description, and it is carried into the export as a to-do.</p>',
        '<h3>5 · Model</h3>',
        '<p>Drag a TMDL folder (<span class="mono">*.SemanticModel/definition/tables</span>) onto the drop area, or click “Demo model” for a quick start. Only names, types and descriptions are read; nothing leaves the browser.</p>',
        '<h3>6 · Export</h3>',
        '<p>“Export for Claude Code” produces <span class="mono">mockup-spec.json</span>, <span class="mono">AGENT-BRIEF.md</span>, <span class="mono">WORKSHOP-DOKU.md</span> and one <span class="mono">pbir-visuals.&lt;page&gt;.json</span> per page. Put the files into the PBIP folder and start the <span class="mono">mockup-to-powerbi</span> skill; it builds the pages and can create the documentation and a PowerPoint from them.</p>',
        '<p>To continue later use <b>Save</b> (<span class="mono">.mockup.json</span>) and <b>Open</b>; the export files are meant for the build, not for loading back.</p>',
        '<h3>7 · Terms</h3>',
        '<p><b>Data role</b>: a slot in the tile that takes a field from the model, e.g. actual value, reference or category. Required roles carry an asterisk.</p>',
        '<p><b>Scenario</b>: which values the tile compares. <b>AC</b> (actual) is the actual value, <b>PL</b> the plan, <b>PY</b> the prior-year value, <b>FC</b> the forecast, <b>BU</b> the budget. “AC/PL” means actual vs plan.</p>',
        '<p><b>Δ basis</b>: the comparison value the variance is calculated against (PL, PY, BU or FC). “auto” follows the bound reference.</p>',
        '<p><b>Δ type</b>: whether the variance is shown as absolute (difference in the unit), relative in percent, or both.</p>',
        '<p><b>Polarity</b>: whether a higher value is good (revenue) or bad (costs). It decides which variance is coloured as favourable.</p>',
        '<p><b>Scale group</b>: tiles with the same group name share one axis scale so sizes stay comparable.</p>',
        '<p><b>Key message</b>: the sentence the tile should tell the reader, e.g. “Revenue 8 % above plan”. It serves as the message title.</p>',
        '<p><b>Engine</b> (implementation): what the tile is built with in Power BI. <b>ChartKitchen</b> is the custom visual by DatenWG with notation inspired by IBCS, <b>Native</b> a standard Power BI visual, <b>Deneb</b> a freely designed Vega-Lite chart in the Deneb visual, <b>Custom</b> another custom visual by DatenWG, such as Gantt or P&amp;L.</p>',
        '<p><b>Badges</b> on the tile: <b>CK</b> ChartKitchen, <b>PBI</b> Power BI standard visual, <b>Deneb</b> Deneb visual, <b>CV</b> custom visual by DatenWG. <b>M</b>, <b>S</b> and <b>C</b> show the priority by MoSCoW (Must, Should, Could). ✎ is a note, <b>?</b> an open question, a red dot an empty required role or a field missing in the model, a coloured dot the workshop status.</p>',
        '<p><b>Spec and export</b>: The spec (<span class="mono">mockup-spec.json</span>) records pages, tiles, fields and analysis settings in machine-readable form. Together with the agent brief and the workshop doc it is the hand-over to the skill that builds the Power BI pages from it.</p>',
        '<p class="hint">Note: The ChartKitchen sketches use a semantic notation; some concepts (scenario marking, variance display) are inspired by IBCS®. MockupKitchen is neither certified by nor affiliated with the IBCS Association; IBCS® is a registered trademark of the IBCS Association.</p>',
        '<h3>Keyboard</h3>',
        '<p><kbd>Del</kbd> clears the selected tile, an empty tile is removed · <kbd>Esc</kbd> clears the selection or ends the presentation · <kbd>N</kbd> opens the tile window of the selected tile · <kbd>P</kbd> presentation on/off · <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Z</kbd> undo · <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Y</kbd> or <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>Z</kbd> redo · <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>S</kbd> save · <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>E</kbd> export · <kbd>F1</kbd> or <kbd>?</kbd> this help. <kbd>Tab</kbd> moves to the tiles, <kbd>Space</kbd> selects the tile, <kbd>Enter</kbd> opens the visual picker on an empty tile. The state is remembered in the browser automatically.</p>',
      ].join(''),
    },

